name wx-crosswind-1
timeout 700

# THE WIND ACTS PHYSICALLY, and this mission is the instrument that proves it — a measurement, not a
# scenario. One jet, one long straight leg due NORTH, and a 20 kt wind FROM 270 (i.e. blowing east), so
# the whole wind vector is across the flight path and none of it along.
#
# WHAT IT PROVES. Nothing in FlightBox tells the pilot, the autopilot or the FCS that there is wind:
# `wx` reaches core/FBWeatherProvider, the unit's OWNER samples it and hands the vector to JSBSim's
# FGWinds (fdm/FBFdm::SetWindNedMs), and JSBSim subtracts it from the ground velocity to get the
# aerodynamic one. So the aircraft ends up CRABBED into the wind purely because its guidance is steering
# at a point on the ground and the air is carrying it sideways:
#
#   drift = heading - ground track ~= asin(crosswind / TAS)
#   groundspeed = sqrt(TAS^2 - crosswind^2)  (crabbed, so the drift is fully cancelled)
#
# Both numbers come out of telemetry.csv against the same file from the run WITHOUT the `wx` line —
# heading is hdgDeg, track and groundspeed are differentiated from lat/lon at the 10 Hz sample rate.
#
# `wx wind` is deliberately the crudest possible atmosphere: one vector everywhere, at every altitude,
# no shear, no boundary layer. That is what makes it an instrument — the expected answer is a closed
# form. Real weather is `wx fixture` (doc/missions/weather.md).

wx wind 270 20

unit viper
  module f16
  spawn 46.30000 7.00000 3000 0.0 350        # air start, due north at 3000 m, wind square on the left
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set datalink off                           # nothing here is an engagement; keep the trace clean
  set fcr_mode off
  wp 46.90000 7.00000 3000 350               # 67 km straight north — the leg the drift is measured on
  wp 47.40000 7.00000 3000 350               # and 56 km more, so the steady state has time to be steady
